  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe remnants of Isaias will continue to track through the region\nearly this morning and into the early afternoon. Rainfall\namounts have ranged from a quarter to near an inch over the \nlast 12 hours, with higher amounts generally focused along the \nridges. The heaviest rainfall amounts will likely occur from now\nthrough 12pm with deep moisture convergence. There is a noted \ndry slot to our west that his created a drastic cut-off in \nrainfall intensity in central Ohio. Overall, rainfall totals\n(through early Monday) will range for 1 to 2 inches. Given our\ndrier conditions leading up to this event and the probability \nof exceeding 6 hour flash flood guidance is considered low (less\nthan 20%), the concern for flooding remains low. Clogged storm \ndrains for falling leaves would be the main concern in isolated\nareas.\n\nWindy conditions along the ridges will continue as low \npressure approaches from the southwest. This will force ridge- \nperpendicular south- easterlies in the Laurel Mountains and high\nterrain of West Virgina, with most recent model guidance having\nthe ridgetops gusting upwards of 40mph over the Laurel \nHighlands. A Wind Advisory remains in effect for the potential \nof falling tree limbs and isolated impacts to the power grid.\nProbability of gusty conditions decreases between 1pm and 4pm \ntoday. \n\nThe probability of rain will decrease from north to south as \nthe trough exits east late this evening, mainly between 7pm and\n10pm. However, there might be some light drizzle and/or rain \nresiding along the ridges early Monday morning with upsloping as\nwinds shift from the northwest. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLong-range models continue to support a large-scale trough \npattern over the Great Lakes late this week. Gusty rain showers\nalong a cold front and plunging temperatures are expected with\nthis system. This pattern could support the end to the growing \nseason.",
